 ## Background
-The concept of early termination in volume rendering has evolved from simple opacity thresholds to sophisticated probabilistic and uncertainty-aware methods. Early implementations relied on deterministic checks against accumulated opacity or transmittance to skip remaining samples [S1]. The introduction of Russian Roulette addressed the bias inherent in deterministic cutoffs by providing an unbiased statistical alternative [S1]. More recent developments, such as Recursive-NeRF, incorporate uncertainty prediction to dynamically decide when a query point is reliable enough to terminate processing, enabling efficient rendering of complex scenes by clustering uncertain points into sub-networks [S1].
